1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:07,428
This video shows you how to turn each item of an array into a record of its own,

2
00:00:07,428 --> 00:00:10,150
with an Unroll step and with code.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,772
Here's the pipeline from the last video.

4
00:00:12,772 --> 00:00:16,430
Each of its three records holds an array of API calls.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:22,030
To search or count the calls one by one, each call needs to be a record of its own.

6
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:26,602
Add an Unroll step, and name the array field: records.

7
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:33,390
Three records become seventeen: one for each call.

8
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:37,750
Each new record holds one call in records, and nothing else.

9
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:42,029
Turn on the switch "keep the parent record's fields on every element",

10
00:00:42,029 --> 00:00:44,868
and each new record keeps the other fields too.

11
00:00:44,868 --> 00:00:48,350
The six calls of the first record share its bytes_out.

12
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,491
Often you want only the call.

13
00:00:50,491 --> 00:00:52,550
Turn the switch off again.

14
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,365
Each call still sits one level down,

15
00:00:55,365 --> 00:01:00,356
inside records: its fields are named records.eventName and so on.

16
00:01:00,356 --> 00:01:03,505
Add a Rename step from records to dollar.

17
00:01:03,505 --> 00:01:06,178
Dollar is the whole record.

18
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,521
The call becomes the record.

19
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:16,524
Its fields are now the record's own: eventName, not records.eventName.

20
00:01:16,524 --> 00:01:22,070
So later steps, and the destination, read each call as a record of its own.

21
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:24,012
A Code step does the same.

22
00:01:24,012 --> 00:01:27,359
The unroll function makes one record per item.

23
00:01:27,359 --> 00:01:32,306
Its second argument is the same switch: false keeps no other fields.

24
00:01:32,306 --> 00:01:37,470
Then $ = records makes the call the whole record, as the Rename did.

25
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:42,285
The result is the same: seventeen records, each one call.

26
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:49,830
The fields you named keep their types: eventName and errorCode are strings.

27
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:51,347
Save.

28
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,491
Check on learning.

29
00:01:56,691 --> 00:01:59,625
A record's array holds six items.

30
00:01:59,625 --> 00:02:03,071
What does unroll with true give?

31
00:02:07,471 --> 00:02:13,718
Six records, each holding one item, and each keeping the record's other fields.

32
00:02:14,618 --> 00:02:17,977
How do you make an item the whole record?

33
00:02:22,377 --> 00:02:26,038
Rename records to dollar with a Rename step,

34
00:02:26,038 --> 00:02:30,798
or assign it to dollar in code, as in $ = records.

35
00:02:31,698 --> 00:02:34,559
You've finished Write smeltr code.
